{
  "image_name": "1ea48bcd80bf4ecea5db27ac11a04ca6.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how an IgG1 antibody can coordinate several immune mechanisms against tumor cells. The antibody binds tumor antigens on the cancer cell surface and also engages Fc receptors on immune cells, linking innate and adaptive responses. A central theme is that the antibody helps convert the tumor environment from more neutral or suppressive cells into tumor-attacking cells, as indicated by the color change in the legend.\n\nMacrophages appear to be reprogrammed from tumour-associated macrophages toward an “M1-like” state, promoting phagocytosis of antibody-coated tumor cells. NK cells and B cells are shown attacking the coated tumor through antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity, with granzyme B release and complement activation contributing to killing. The diagram also highlights antigen presentation and T-cell priming: CD4+ T cells interact through MHC class II and become helper T cells, while CD8+ T cells are activated through MHC class I and TCR recognition, leading to cytotoxic CD8+ T cells that directly attack the tumor.\n\nCytokines such as IFNγ and IL-12 appear to support communication among these immune cells. Overall, the figure shows an antibody-driven, multi-cell immune cascade that enhances both direct and indirect antitumor activity.",
